{{smaller block|I hold ambition of so ''airy'' and light a quality, that it is but a shadow’s shadow.{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, ii. 2.}}}}
'''5.''' Visionary; speculative: as, ''airy'' notions; an ''airy'' metaphysician.—'''6.''' Graceful; delicate.
{{smaller block|<poem>E’en the slight hare-bell raised its head,
Elastic from her ''airy'' tread.</poem>{{right|''Scott'', L. of the L., i. 18.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Here delicate snow-stars, out of the cloud,
Come floating downward in ''airy'' play.</poem>{{right|''Bryant'', Snow-Shower.}}}}
'''7.''' Light in manner or movement; sprightly; gay; lively.
{{smaller block|It saddens the heart to see a man, from whom nature has withheld all perception of the tones and attitudes of humour, labouring with all his might to be ''airy'' and playful.{{right|''Gifford'', Ford’s Plays, Int., p. xlv.}}}}
{{smaller block|Chaucer works still in the solid material of his race, but with what ''airy'' lightness has he not infused it?{{right|''Lowell'', Study Windows, p. 252.}}}}
'''8.''' Jaunty; full of airs; affectedly lofty; pretentious.—'''9.''' In ''painting'', showing that proper recession of all parts which expresses distance and atmosphere. ='''Syn.''' {{smaller|''Airy'', ''Aërial'', aëriform. ''Airy'' is more open to figurative meanings than ''aërial''. The latter is the more exact word in other respects; it applies to the air as atmosphere: as, ''aërial'' navigation. ''Airy'' applies rather to air in motion, and to that which has the qualities, literal or imagined, of air.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Echo’s no more an empty ''airy'' sound;
But a fair nymph that weeps her lover drown’d.</poem>{{right|''Dryden'', Art of Poetry, iii. 598.}}}}
{{smaller block|We have already discovered the art of coasting along the ''aërial'' shores of our planet, by means of balloons.{{right|''Irving'', Knickerbocker, p. 77.}}}}

'''airy'''<sup>2</sup>† (ãr′i), ''n.'' An old and better spelling of ''aery''<sup>2</sup>.

